# =============================================================================
# THE MODEL FILE FOR THIS PROFILE NO LONGER EXISTS (deleted 2026-08-19)
# =============================================================================
# Step-3.7-Flash-UD-Q4_K_XL-R4-*.gguf was 114 GB beside the 114 GB non-R4 copy
# that step-3.7-flash-q4 uses -- the same model twice, differing only in weight
# layout. It was deleted because on this machine it can only lose.
#
# `_R4` has NO CUDA kernel (0 files under ggml/src/ggml-cuda/, 7 under
# ggml/src/iqk/), so a repack baked into the file pins those experts to the CPU
# permanently -- and RESULTS §21 measured the CPU path losing 3x to the GPU one
# here. The profile ran `-rtr 0` and still computed on the CPU for exactly that
# reason.
#
# The profile is kept as the record of what was measured (§1.6/§1.7). To run it
# again the file has to be recreated with `llama-quantize --repack`, which takes
# hours -- and is only worth doing on a machine where the CPU wins, i.e. a narrow
# PCIe link or a CPU with AVX-512. See TODO item 8.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Step-3.7-Flash -- Q4_K_XL with the CPU-resident experts repacked to Q4_K_R4
# =============================================================================
# A prefill-optimised copy of the Q4 model. Only the routed experts of layers
# 3..21 -- exactly the ones that live on the CPU at ncmoe=22 -- are stored in
# the row-interleaved _R4 layout the AVX2/VNNI CPU kernels prefer. Layers 22..44
# stay plain Q4_K so the GPU can use them.
#
# MEASURED (2026-07-25, ncmoe=22, 262144 ctx, mmap on):
#   prefill    246 tok/s   (vs 208 plain Q4  ->  +18%)
#   generation 26.5 tok/s  (unchanged; generation is bandwidth-bound)
# Same quality as Q4 (COPY = no requantization), same ~114 GiB, mmap stays on
# so startup is normal -- this is the -rtr prefill gain baked in, without -rtr's
# per-launch re-read of the whole model.
#
# Produced with:
#   llama-quantize --repack \
#     --repack-pattern 'blk\.([3-9]|1[0-9]|2[01])\.ffn_.*_exps' --keep-split \
#     Step-3.7-Flash-UD-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00004.gguf \
#     Step-3.7-Flash-UD-Q4_K_XL-R4.gguf COPY 18
#
# !!!  LOCKED TO ncmoe=22 / 262144 CONTEXT  !!! -----------------------------
# The repacked layers are baked into the file. If a layer that is stored as _R4
# ends up on the GPU, inference collapses to ~0.3 tok/s (an _R4 tensor on the
# GPU hits a catastrophic fallback -- measured). So:
#   * DO NOT lower IK_NCMOE below 22 -- layers 3..21 would go on the GPU. Fatal.
#   * Raising IK_NCMOE is safe (more layers on CPU; the extra ones are plain Q4_K
#     and just miss the _R4 speedup). Lowering IK_CTX needs a lower ncmoe, which
#     is exactly what you must not do -- so for shorter contexts use the plain
#     'step-3.7-flash-q4' profile, or re-repack with a matching layer range.
# This is why the plain Q4 profile stays the default; use this one only when you
# do prefill-heavy work (long prompts / RAG) at the 262144 / ncmoe=22 config.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/unsloth/Step-3.7-Flash-GGUF/Step-3.7-Flash-UD-Q4_K_XL-R4-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=step-3.7-flash}"

# These two MUST stay matched to how the file was repacked (layers 3..21 on CPU).
: "${IK_CTX:=262144}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=22}"

# Guard: an _R4 expert placed on the GPU collapses to ~0.3 tok/s. ncmoe below 22
# would put repacked layers (3..21) on the GPU -- refuse it loudly.
if [[ -n ${IK_NCMOE:-} && ${IK_NCMOE} -lt 22 ]]; then
    printf '\033[31mfail\033[0m this R4 model is baked for ncmoe>=22; ncmoe=%s puts _R4 experts on the GPU (~0.3 tok/s). Use the plain step-3.7-flash-q4 profile instead.\n' "$IK_NCMOE" >&2
    exit 1
fi

# The file is already repacked; -rtr would force --no-mmap for no benefit.
: "${IK_RTR:=0}"
